## Method
Everything runs as one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1),
submitted as one batch job. Inputs are read only; nothing is written outside `results/`.

1. **Resolve inputs** at run time with `_system/resolve_artifact.py --type counts_gene` and
   `--type design` for assay `rnaseq_bulk`. Stop with an error if either fails to resolve.
2. **Load counts.** Read the TSV, index it by `gene_id`, drop the `gene_name` column, and keep
   only the sample columns. Stop with an error unless the sample columns and the design's
   `sample_id` values are the same set; then put the design rows in the count-matrix order.
3. **Make integer counts.** These are salmon length-scaled estimates, so they are not integers.
   Round them to the nearest integer for DESeq2 (the usual tximport `countsFromAbundance`
   practice). This happens on an in-memory copy only.
4. **Filter low-count genes.** Keep genes with at least 10 counts in total across all samples.
5. **Variance-stabilise.** Build a PyDESeq2 `DeseqDataSet` from the counts with
   `design="~condition"`, then run `vst()` with `use_design=False`, i.e. blind to condition, so
   the transform does not use the labels the plot is coloured by. Size factors use DESeq2's
   default median-of-ratios method.
6. **Select genes.** Take the 500 genes with the highest row variance of the VST values
   (DESeq2 `plotPCA` default `ntop=500`), or every gene if fewer than 500 remain.
7. **PCA.** Centre each selected gene across samples (no scaling) and run
   `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` with `n_components = min(n_samples, n_genes)` (= 6 here) and
   `svd_solver="full"`. Record every sample's scores on all components, plus each component's
   explained-variance ratio.
8. **Plot.** Draw a PC1 vs PC2 scatter, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` with a
   fixed two-colour categorical palette and a legend. Label each point with its `sample_id`.
   Axis titles give the percentage of variance explained, e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`. Save it as PNG at
   300 dpi and as PDF.
9. **Write tables.** Write the per-sample scores with the design columns joined on, and the
   per-component variance explained (see Outputs).

